1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,156
This is one bore, machined once. It has exactly one size.

2
00:00:05,250 --> 00:00:08,990
One size, and nothing has been measured yet.

3
00:00:09,083 --> 00:00:14,534
Measure it once, and again, and two hundred times. The bore has not changed.

4
00:00:14,634 --> 00:00:19,063
The readings have a shape, a centre and a spread of their own.

5
00:00:19,150 --> 00:00:23,335
One true size. Seven microns of spread on the screen.

6
00:00:23,435 --> 00:00:27,378
That spread belongs to the gauge, not to the part.

7
00:00:29,066 --> 00:00:32,326
So a reading is never just the part.

8
00:00:32,416 --> 00:00:34,949
It is the part, plus whatever the

9
00:00:35,049 --> 00:00:38,220
measurement process did on that occasion.

10
00:00:38,316 --> 00:00:41,469
Watch the error, not the part. The part is

11
00:00:41,569 --> 00:00:44,799
fixed. The error is a draw from the gauge's

12
00:00:44,899 --> 00:00:49,136
own distribution, and it lands somewhere new every time.

13
00:00:49,233 --> 00:00:53,861
That distribution is the thing this whole subject studies.

14
00:00:55,450 --> 00:01:00,366
If the reading is the part plus an error, the spread you observe must

15
00:01:00,466 --> 00:01:05,238
be wider than the spread of the parts. The question is by how much.

16
00:01:05,333 --> 00:01:09,025
The tempting answer is that the two spreads add.

17
00:01:09,116 --> 00:01:12,962
It is not. Independent variation adds the way

18
00:01:13,062 --> 00:01:15,856
the sides of a right triangle do.

19
00:01:15,950 --> 00:01:20,578
The variances add. The standard deviations do not.

20
00:01:20,666 --> 00:01:24,833
Four point seven and one point four give four point

21
00:01:24,933 --> 00:01:28,765
nine, not six point one. The gauge is a quarter

22
00:01:28,865 --> 00:01:32,446
of the part spread and it costs 4.3 percent.

23
00:01:34,233 --> 00:01:37,141
Sweep the gauge from perfect to as bad as

24
00:01:37,241 --> 00:01:40,517
the parts themselves, and watch what it costs.

25
00:01:40,616 --> 00:01:44,485
A gauge at a tenth of the part spread costs half a

26
00:01:44,585 --> 00:01:49,168
percent. At a third it costs four. The curve is almost flat

27
00:01:49,268 --> 00:01:53,692
here, which is why a mediocre gauge often goes unnoticed.

28
00:01:53,783 --> 00:01:56,775
Then it bends. A gauge as noisy as the

29
00:01:56,875 --> 00:02:00,763
parts inflates what you see by forty one percent.

30
00:02:00,850 --> 00:02:04,677
Read it the other way and it is uncomfortable. Your

31
00:02:04,777 --> 00:02:08,604
gauge may be nearly half the part spread before the

32
00:02:08,704 --> 00:02:13,302
histogram is ten percent too wide. Small effects hide easily.

33
00:02:15,100 --> 00:02:19,320
The obvious move is to measure each part several times

34
00:02:19,420 --> 00:02:22,680
and average. It works, and it has a floor.

35
00:02:22,766 --> 00:02:27,712
Averaging divides the measurement variance by the number of repeats. It

36
00:02:27,812 --> 00:02:31,833
does nothing at all to the variation between the parts, so

37
00:02:31,933 --> 00:02:35,386
the curve falls towards the part spread and stops.

38
00:02:35,483 --> 00:02:40,251
Five repeats gets you within one percent of the floor and twenty-five

39
00:02:40,351 --> 00:02:44,838
barely improves on that. So the honest question is never how good

40
00:02:44,938 --> 00:02:48,789
is my gauge. It is how big is it, compared to something.

41
00:02:48,889 --> 00:02:53,023
Level two asks whose spread we have actually been measuring.

